exercises.solution:
Input power is $P_\text{out}/\eta$, so the annual saving is $P_\text{out}\,t\,(1/\eta_\text{old} - 1/\eta_\text{new})$.

```python
def motor_savings(p_out_kw, hours, eff_old, eff_new, price, extra_cost):
    kwh = p_out_kw * hours * (1 / eff_old - 1 / eff_new)
    if kwh <= 0:
        return 0.0, float('inf')
    return kwh, extra_cost / (kwh * price)
```

For 15 kW running 6000 hours a year, raising the efficiency from 91% to 94.5% saves about 3660 kWh a year and pays back an extra 600 in about 13 months at 0.15 per kWh, and the motor will run for fifteen years or more. Small efficiency differences matter because motors run for so long, which is why minimum efficiency standards for motors are among the most cost-effective climate policies.
